    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Brice Turang** (73.40) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (67.04) anchor our lineup, offering the most compelling efficiency profiles against favorable matchups. **Brice Turang** benefits immensely from the high xERA of Lowder, pairing a significant SP Skill boost (+19%) with a top-of-order anchor (+6% for batting 3rd). Similarly, **Tyler Soderstrom** draws a plus matchup against Mahle; despite the park downgrade, the dynamic platoon advantage and a clean spot in the heart of the order keep his ceiling high. **Corbin Carroll** (66.84) remains a mandatory start; while he battles a minor platoon disadvantage against Liberatore, his elite xwOBA metrics and the premium on his speed profile solidify his role as a daily pillar for Ghost Ride the WHIP.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Zach McKinstry**, who finished just shy of the active roster with a score of 46.90. While his matchup against Weathers offered a useful platoon lift and a rare \"Slow SP Delivery\" boost to his steal probability, he ultimately lacked the raw efficiency to displace **Kyle Karros** or **Max Muncy**. The depth of our UTIL spot remains a high-variance area, but the data suggests that **Kyle Karros**, despite a bottom-of-the-order penalty, maintains a higher floor due to an extreme park factor advantage.\n\nWe are proceeding with caution regarding the top of the order for **James Wood** (62.42) and **Jose Caballero** (51.82). Both face elite arms in Nola and Skubal, respectively, which heavily dampens their base projections. While **James Wood** earns his spot through elite xwOBA and stolen base upside, the presence of an elite catcher\u2014who significantly depresses the probability of successful swipes\u2014serves as a lingering risk. Owners should monitor the weather closely for any late-breaking system impacts that could force a pivot away from these high-leverage, low-probability matchups.",
